Severe slugging in pipeline--riser systems combines phase disappearance, moving liquid and gas fronts, changes of flow pattern and cycles of hundreds of seconds, which makes it a demanding test for transient two-fluid simulators. This work asks what a one-dimensional, four-equation two-fluid model with pattern-dependent closures needs to reproduce the cycle when the flow pattern is identified dynamically along the whole system, and uses the answer on a well--flowline--riser system of field dimensions (a \SI{1500}{\metre} well, a \SI{2}{\kilo\metre} flowline and a \SI{317}{\metre} riser) with a topside choke. A characteristic analysis shows that the closures are ill-posed in large parts of their domain; a low-void rule, an objective virtual-mass term and an interfacial pressure in the slug unit leave the model hyperbolic in all but \SI{0.1}{\percent} of the states visited by a severe-slugging simulation, against \SI{29}{\percent} without the virtual mass. The slug unit of the intermittent closure must take its film from the physics of the film rather than from the mass balance of the cell, or the unit is sold short at the triple point where the slug, the film and the cell hold the same gas, and the bubbly criteria of the pattern identification must read the void fraction of the cell rather than the gas flow rate. Only a staggered finite-volume scheme with phase-wise donor cells carries the cycle; integrating the non-conservative form loses liquid at every blowout, whereas a conservative residual closes the mass balances to the Newton tolerance, and evaluating the face closures with the donor void fraction removes an odd--even mode and an unstable oscillation of the cells at the bend. A variable-step implicit integrator with physical bounds, range-scaled tolerances, a line search that stops at convergence and a second-order error estimate, driven by a compiled right-hand side with exact Jacobians, runs four hours of slugging in four to eight minutes. Linearised with the mass matrix of the conservative formulation, the steady state loses stability through two complex pairs --- the riser column oscillating against the gas pocket, and the exchange of liquid between flowline and riser, whose period is that of the simulated cycles --- and, with the choke open, through a real mode: the onset of slugging is a Hopf bifurcation, located at a choke opening where the pressure drop across the choke is \SI{3.7}{\bar} against a riser head of \SI{20}{\bar}, as the criterion of Jansen and co-workers predicts. On the field case the cycle is born at an opening of 0.19 as a pressure cycle of \SI{700}{\second}, grows continuously into relaxation oscillations and doubles its period between openings of 0.6 and 0.7; with the choke open the blowouts alternate in size, 33 and \SI{45}{\kilogram\per\second} at intervals that do not repeat within the run, against a mean production of \SI{3.9}{\kilogram\per\second} and the flowline pressure swinging between 15 and \SI{31}{\bar}; closing the choke to 0.1 stabilises the riser at the cost of \SI{7}{\percent} of the production, and the gas--oil ratio of the well sets the period of the cycle but neither its pressure range nor its surge. A gas-lift branch --- line, annulus and check valve, simulated with the same model and controlled only by the gas pressure at the platform --- shortens and regularises the cycle between 100 and \SI{120}{\bar}, with the injection modulated by the cycle, and suppresses it from \SI{130}{\bar}, at an injected rate of \SI{0.9}{\kilogram\per\second}, seven times the free gas of the well, with \SI{29}{\percent} more production.
